<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Communards</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Communards were the supporters and defenders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a>, the revolutionary government that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from March to May 1871. Thousands of them were killed during the 'Bloody Week' of May 1871, when government troops retook the city, and many more were imprisoned or deported to New Caledonia, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louise-michel">Louise Michel</a>. An amnesty allowed the survivors to return in 1880.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/communards.jpg" alt="Communards" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bruno Braquehais · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barricade_Voltaire_Lenoir_Commune_Paris_1871.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Prussian siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from September 1870 left the poor eating cat or rat meat or going hungry, and radical and socialist clubs and newspapers grew out of their resentment. On 18 March 1871 the government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> sent troops to collect a reserve of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, but units of the Paris National Guard surrounded them and killed two generals, and by the end of the day the insurgents held the city and declared the Commune. Adolphe Thiers refused to bargain with them, and the Communards built barricades.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the army retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, 43,000 Communards were taken prisoner and 6,500 to 7,500 fled abroad. The number killed in combat or executed has long been disputed: Prosper-Olivier Lissagaray gave 20,000, while more recent historians put it between 10,000 and 15,000. A government inquest blamed the rising chiefly on a lack of belief in God, and some 4,500 were deported to New Caledonia, where the Isle of Pines was set aside for them. About two-thirds received simple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a>, while about 300 sentenced to forced labour were held with criminal convicts and suffered whippings and thumbscrews.</p><p>Food, clothing and shelter were short, and many built their own huts and traded clothes with the Kanak for food. In 1874 six déportés, among them François Jourde and Henri Rochefort, escaped by ship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a>, after which the rules grew stricter. Some Communards, such as Achille Ballière, formed friendships with the Kanak, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louise-michel">Louise Michel</a> ran a school for them and supported those who joined the Kanak insurrection of 1878.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In January 1879 the prime minister Jules Armand Dufaure granted mass pardons, though more than one thousand were left off the list. Louis Blanc and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a> introduced bills for a total amnesty, which parliament voted in July 1880, and nine ships brought the déportés home between August 1879 and July 1880 to celebrating crowds. Some stayed in New Caledonia or settled in countries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. An inquiry into torture in New Caledonia from December 1879 put the Communards' accounts of abuse on the public record.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Communards” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between 1864 and 1897 France transported about 22,000 convicts, among them deported <b>Communards</b> such as Louise Michel.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-caledonia">New Caledonia</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Communards</b> also pulled down the Vendôme Column.</blockquote><a href="/state/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a></li></ul></section>
